# HD Format v2 (Markdown-primary)

**HD version 2** stores document content as **Markdown first, HTML only where Markdown can't express the intent**. It is **now the default**: new documents are created as v2, and every save writes v2 — regardless of file extension. The older body-only-HTML format is version 1, still readable and migrated to v2 the first time you edit it. (The `.hd2` extension was the original home of this format and still opens; it is now a deprecated alias for a v2 `.hd` file.)

**Status: default format.** Version 2 has replaced version 1 as the format new content is created and saved in. A legacy v1 (`.hd`, HTML) file opens unchanged and is migrated to v2 on its first edit — the `version:` frontmatter field, not the file extension, records which version a file is. There is no separate `.hd2` format to consolidate anymore; it is simply an alternate extension for the same v2 content.

## The one-sentence difference

Version 1 stores **body-only HTML** on disk; version 2 stores **Markdown with raw-HTML islands** on disk. Everything else — the editor, the frontmatter, the asset model, the document identity — is shared.

## What's identical to HD

-   **The editor.** The same VS Code WYSIWYG editor opens both. Typing, tables, paste, image handling, and the toolbar are unchanged.
    
-   **Frontmatter.** The same bare `---` YAML fence, the same fields (`id`, `title`, `date`, `version`, …).
    
-   **Document identity.** The same 22-character base62 `id`, injected on first open.
    
-   **Assets.** The same `.hd/<id>/` asset folders, keyed by `id`. HD2 deliberately reuses the existing asset directory so a document can move between flavors without touching its assets.
    
-   **The editing capability set.** Anything you can author in HD, you can author in HD2 — merged table cells, inline SVG, styled spans, and so on. The difference is only how those things are *serialized to disk*.
    

## What's different from HD

-   **On-disk representation.** Prose, headings, lists, simple tables, and other Markdown-expressible content are stored as Markdown. Everything Markdown can't represent is stored as a verbatim HTML island.
    
-   **Frontmatter version.** v2 files carry `version: 2`; legacy v1 files carry `version: 1`. The field is the source of truth for how the body is stored.
    
-   **Cleaner diffs, more familiar source.** The raw file reads like Markdown and produces smaller, line-oriented git diffs for prose edits.
    
-   **A first-save reformat.** Because the editor re-serializes to a single canonical Markdown style, the first save of a hand-written or imported file may reflow it once. See [How HD2 Works](how-it-works.hd).
    

## In this section

-   [How HD2 Works](how-it-works.hd) — the Markdown-primary model, the conversion boundary, the load/save round-trip, and the caveats that come with it.
    
-   [HD2 Capabilities](capabilities.hd) — the element-by-element map of what is stored as Markdown versus what becomes an HTML island, including the exact rules for tables and images.
    
-   [Interactive Controls](interactive-controls.hd) — clickable checkboxes and radio buttons, the markdown syntax for each, how selection state is saved, and the agent review-and-approve workflow they enable.
